## [ISSUE] Client-side category diverges from the server default after save

**Severity:** LOW — After saving a host with no category chosen, the client sets `host.category` to `findById(null)` (undefined) while the server stored `uncategorized_category_id`, so the row shows no category badge until reload.

### The Problem
```js
host.save(props).then(() => {
  host.set('category', Discourse.Category.findById(this.get('categoryId')));
```
When the category chooser is left empty, `categoryId` is `null`. The server replaces a blank value with `SiteSetting.uncategorized_category_id` (`embeddable_hosts_controller.rb:26`) and returns that id in the response.

### Theory / Proof
`Discourse.Category.findById(null)` returns `undefined`, so `{{category-badge host.category}}` renders nothing. `RestModel#save` updates the record's `category_id` from the response payload, but the component overwrites `category` with the stale client value.

### What happens today vs. what will break
A new host is stored in Uncategorized but the admin table shows a blank category cell, which looks like the category was not saved. Reloading the page fixes the display.

### Research
The server already returns the authoritative `category_id`, so the component should use it.

### Recommendation
```js
host.save(props).then(() => {
  host.set('category', Discourse.Category.findById(host.get('category_id')));
  this.set('editToggled', false);
}).catch(popupAjaxError);
```

## [ISSUE] `_ids` hydration can produce `undefined` array entries

**Severity:** LOW — The new `_ids` branch maps ids through `_lookupSubType` without filtering, so ids missing from the side-loaded collection become `undefined` array entries (the `|| []` is dead code), and templates iterating the array will crash on them.

### The Problem
```js
const hydrated = obj[k].map(function(id) {
  return self._lookupSubType(subType, type, id, root);
});
obj[self.pluralize(subType)] = hydrated || [];
```
`_lookupSubType` returns `undefined` when `root[pluralType]` is absent or does not contain the id. `Array#map` always returns an array, so `hydrated || []` never falls back.

### Theory / Proof
The singular branch keeps `obj[k]` when lookup fails (`if (hydrated) {...}`). The plural branch deletes `obj[k]` unconditionally and stores `[model, undefined, ...]`. It also throws if `obj[k]` is `null`.

### What happens today vs. what will break
With `EmbeddingSerializer` (`embed :ids, include: true`) every id is side-loaded, so this works today. Any future endpoint that returns `*_ids` without side-loading the collection, or with a partial collection, puts `undefined` into the array. `{{#each}}` and component code such as `host.isNew` then fail.

### Research
The singular path in the same function already handles a missing lookup defensively. The plural path should do the same.

### Recommendation
```js
if (m[2]) {
  const ids = obj[k] || [];
  obj[self.pluralize(subType)] = ids
    .map(id => self._lookupSubType(subType, type, id, root))
    .filter(Boolean);
  delete obj[k];
}
```
